{"boost_definition":"Intel boost_ghz is the ARK Max Turbo Frequency (the headline 'up to' figure). For K-series chips this includes Thermal Velocity Boost / Turbo Boost Max 3.0, so it is higher than the P-core-only Max Turbo (for example i7-13700K: Max Turbo 5.4 GHz, P-core Max Turbo 5.3 GHz). AMD boost_ghz is the Max Boost Clock. e_core_boost_ghz is Intel's E-core turbo figure: Efficient-core Max Turbo Frequency (12th-14th gen), Single E-core Turbo Frequency (Core Ultra desktop), Low Power Efficient-core Max Turbo Frequency (Core Ultra 200V, which has no standard E-cores). A rated boost clock is a spec-sheet maximum, not a guaranteed sustained clock: it depends on thermals, power limits and core count.","coverage":"74 CPUs, 53 GPUs in v1.1.0: Intel Core 8th-14th gen desktop, Core Ultra 100/200 desktop and mobile, Intel 8th/10th/11th-gen mobile U/G/H; AMD Ryzen 1000-9000 desktop and Ryzen 3000-8000 plus Ryzen AI 300 mobile. GPUs: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 50/40/30-series desktop, RTX 40/30-series laptop, GTX 1660/1660 SUPER/1660 Ti/1650 desktop, GTX 1060 6GB and GTX 1080 desktop; AMD Radeon RX 6000/7000/9000 desktop. NOT included: 12th/13th-gen Intel mobile H/P chips (Intel publishes no per-SKU P-core base frequency for them, so their rows cannot be completed without inference), i3-8100 (no Turbo Boost, so no boost figure exists), i5-14400, i9-11980HK, Ryzen 7 3700U (no verifiable manufacturer page found); RTX 4060 Ti and RTX 3080 desktop (board power split between variants with no mapping on the NVIDIA page), RTX 3050 desktop (boost, memory and power all differ between its 8 GB and 6 GB variants under one device name), GTX 1060 3GB (no power figure on nvidia.com), GTX 1070 and GTX 1080 Ti (no boost or power figure on nvidia.com), RTX 20-series and GTX 16-series laptop GPUs (NVIDIA publishes no laptop spec tables for them), all AMD laptop GPUs (AMD publishes Game Frequency, not Boost Frequency, for them), Intel Arc A580/A750/A770 (no manufacturer spec page locatable). A mo